    "status": "高度验证/即将临床",
    "sample": "CSF/血浆",
    "finding": "【证据1·Nature structural & molecular biology 2025】(PMID 40841486) Proteomic profiling of Alzheimer disease (AD) brains has identified numerous understudied proteins, including midkine (MDK), that are highly upregulated and correlated with amyloid-β (Aβ) from the early disease stage but their roles in disease progression are not fully understood. Knockout of the Mdk gene in 5xFAD increased amyloid formation and microglial activation in the brain.｜【证据2·Cell reports. Medicine 2024】(PMID 39127040) AD brain proteomics reveals vast alterations in protein levels and numerous altered biologic pathways. Proteins in the most conserved network (M42) accumulate in plaques, cerebrovascular amyloid (CAA), and/or dystrophic neuronal processes, and overexpression of two M42 proteins, midkine (Mdk) and pleiotrophin (PTN), increases the accumulation of Aβ in plaques and CAA.｜【证据3·Cancer letters 2004】(PMID 15013213) During embryogenesis, MK is highly expressed in the mid-gestational period, whereas PTN expression reaches the maximum level around birth. Both proteins are localized in the radial glial processes of the embryonic brain, along which neural stem cells migrate and differentiate.",
